<http://dbpedia.org/resource/Michigan> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#comment> "Michigan (/ˈmɪʃɪɡən/) is a state in the Great Lakes region of the upper Midwestern United States. With a population of nearly 10.12 million and an area of nearly 97,000 sq mi (250,000 km2), Michigan is the 10th-largest state by population, the 11th-largest by area, and the largest by area east of the Mississippi River. Its capital is Lansing, and its largest city is Detroit. Metro Detroit is among the nation's most populous and largest metropolitan economies. Its name derives from a gallicized variant of the original Ojibwe word ᒥᓯᑲᒥ (mishigami), meaning \"large water\" or \"large lake\"."@en .
<http://dbpedia.org/resource/Sedona,_Arizona> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#comment> "**Sedona**  is a city that straddles the county line between [Coconino](:Coconino_County%2C_Arizona) and [Yavapai](:Yavapai_County%2C_Arizona) counties in the northern [Verde Valley](:Verde_Valley) region of the [U.S. state](:U%2ES%2E_state) of [Arizona](:Arizona). As of the [2010 census](:2010_United_States_Census), its population was 10,031. It is within the [Coconino National Forest](:Coconino_National_Forest).Sedona's main attraction is its array of red [sandstone](:Sandstone) formations. The formations appear to glow in brilliant orange and red when illuminated by the rising or setting sun. The red rocks form a popular backdrop for many activities, ranging from spiritual pursuits to the hundreds of hiking and mountain biking trails. Sedona is also the home to the nationally recognized [McDonald's](:McDonald%27s) with turquoise arches, instead of the traditional Golden Arches.Sedona was named after [Sedona Arabella Miller Schnebly](:Sedona_Schnebly) (1877–1950), the wife of Theodore Carlton Schnebly, the city's first postmaster. She was celebrated for her hospitality and industriousness. Her mother, Amanda Miller, claimed to have made the name up because \"it [sounded pretty](:Phonaesthetics)\"."^^<http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string> .
