<http://dbpedia.org/resource/Reading,_Pennsylvania> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#comment> "**Reading** ( [*RED-ing*](:Help%3APronunciation_respelling_key); [Pennsylvania Dutch](:Pennsylvania_Dutch_language): *Reddin*) is a city in and the [county seat](:County_seat) of [Berks County, Pennsylvania](:Berks_County%2C_Pennsylvania), United States. The city had a population of 95,112 as of the [2020 census](:2020_United_States_census) and is [the fourth-largest city in Pennsylvania](:List_of_cities_and_boroughs_in_Pennsylvania_by_population) after [Philadelphia](:Philadelphia), [Pittsburgh](:Pittsburgh), and [Allentown](:Allentown%2C_Pennsylvania). Reading is located in the southeastern part of the state and is the principal city of the [Greater Reading Area](:Berks_County%2C_Pennsylvania), which had 420,152 residents as of 2020.Reading is part of the [Delaware Valley](:Delaware_Valley), also known as the Philadelphia metropolitan area, a region that also includes [Philadelphia](:Philadelphia), [Upper Darby Township, Pennsylvania](:Upper_Darby_Township%2C_Delaware_County%2C_Pennsylvania), [Camden](:Camden%2C_New_Jersey), and other suburban Philadelphia cities and regions. With a 2020 population of 6,228,601, the Delaware Valley is the [seventh largest metropolitan region in the nation](:Metropolitan_statistical_area) as of 2020.The origin of Reading's name comes from the now-defunct [Reading Company](:Reading_Company), widely known as the Reading Railroad and since acquired by [Conrail](:Conrail), that played a vital role in transporting [anthracite](:Anthracite) coal from the Pennsylvania's [Coal Region](:Coal_Region) to major East Coast metropolitan markets through the [Port of Philadelphia](:Port_of_Philadelphia) for much of the 19th and 20th centuries. Reading Railroad is one of the four railroad properties in the classic U.S. version of the *[Monopoly](:Monopoly_%28game%29)* board game. Reading was one of the first localities where outlet shopping became a tourist industry. It has been known as \"The Pretzel City\" because numerous local [pretzel](:Pretzel) bakeries are based in the city and it suburbs; currently, Bachman, Dieffenbach, Tom Sturgis, and Unique Pretzel bakeries call the Reading area home. In recent years, the Reading area has become a destination for cyclists with more than 125 miles of trails in five major preserves; the region is an [International Mountain Bicycling Association](:International_Mountain_Bicycling_Association) ride center.According to 2010 U.S. Census data, Reading had the highest share of citizens living in poverty in the nation among cities with populations exceeding 65,000. Reading's poverty rate fell over the next decade. Reading's poverty rate in the five-year [American Community Survey](:American_Community_Survey), published in 2018, showed that 35.4% of the city's residents were below the poverty line, or less \"than the infamous 41.3% from 2011, when Reading was declared the poorest small city in the nation.\"Reading is located 38.8 miles (62.4 km) southwest of Allentown and 62.9 miles (101.2 km) northwest of Philadelphia."^^<http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string> .
