<http://dbpedia.org/resource/Piedras_Negras,_Coahuila> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#comment> "**Piedras Negras** (Template:IPA-es is a city and seat of the [surrounding municipality of the same name](:Piedras_Negras_Municipality) in the Mexican [state](:List_of_states_of_Mexico) of [Coahuila](:Coahuila). It stands at the northeastern edge of Coahuila on the [Mexico–United States border](:Mexico%E2%80%93United_States_border), across the [Rio Grande](:Rio_Grande) from [Eagle Pass](:Eagle_Pass%2C_Texas) in the U.S. state of [Texas](:Texas). In the 2015 census the city had a population of 163,595 inhabitants, while the metropolitan area had a population of 245,155 inhabitants. The Piedras Negras and the Eagle Pass areas are connected by the [Eagle Pass–Piedras Negras International Bridge](:Eagle_Pass%E2%80%93Piedras_Negras_International_Bridge), [Camino Real International Bridge](:Camino_Real_International_Bridge), and the [Union Pacific International Railroad Bridge](:Union_Pacific_International_Railroad_Bridge_%28Eagle_Pass%E2%80%93Piedras_Negras%29).In Spanish, *Piedras Negras* translates to 'black stones' – a reference to coal deposits in the area. Across the river, coal was formerly mined on the U.S. side at Dolchburg, near Eagle Pass. This mine closed around 1905, after a fire. Mexico currently operates two large [coal-fired power station](:Coal-fired_power_station) named \"[José López Portillo](:Jos%C3%A9_L%C3%B3pez_Portillo)\" and \"Carbón 2\" located 30 miles (48 km) south of Piedras Negras. These two coal-fired power plants are currently operated by [Comisión Federal de Electricidad](:Comisi%C3%B3n_Federal_de_Electricidad), the state-owned Mexican [electric utility](:Electric_utility)."^^<http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string> .
